sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch10-05> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Once a few simple programs corresponding to standard methods of perception and analysis have failed to detect regularities in some data, it is extremely rare for any other simple program to succeed, so the everyday judgment of randomness is in practice fairly unambiguous.";
    dc:type "computational-observation";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "If a few standard tests find no regularity, other simple programs rarely do";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 10, “Processes of Perception and Analysis”; section “Defining the Notion of Randomness” (from p. 552), p. 556. Scope: detection of regularities in sequences and images by simple programs"
}
